Testing the `conformance.kernel.recovery` rule's handling of JSON objects with repeated keys, specifically whether the rule's 'last-wins' resolution for repeated keys is correctly applied when the last occurrence of a key is nested inside a later top-level object. The rule states: 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text... A candidate that parses as strict JSON, or as JSON with repeated keys resolved last-wins, is scoreable.' The input contains two top-level objects, where the second object repeats a key from the first but with a different value. The rewritten version moves the repeated key to a nested object inside the second top-level object, which should still be the one scored with the last-wins resolution. The expectation is that the check will move ('moves') because the last occurrence of the repeated key changes from the second top-level object to the nested object within it, testing whether the rule's 'last-wins' applies to nested objects or only to top-level keys.